      "page_title": "Why Transactions Exist in MongoDB",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Atomic By Default",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A write to ONE document is always atomic, however deeply nested",
            "Embedding is the first-choice transaction — atomicity for free",
            "Transactions are an escape hatch, not the default access pattern",
            "Design goal: make multi-document transactions rarely necessary"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Motivating Case"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const opt = { session }",
            "await acc.updateOne(a,{$inc:{bal:-100}},opt)",
            "await acc.updateOne(b,{$inc:{bal:+100}},opt)",
            "// two docs: both land, or neither does"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "ACID, MongoDB-Shaped"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Letter",
            "What it means here"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "A",
              "all writes commit or none"
            ],
            [
              "C",
              "schema + index constraints hold"
            ],
            [
              "I",
              "snapshot reads"
            ],
            [
              "D",
              "write concern majority + journal"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Version Timeline"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Release",
            "Transactions across"
          ],
          "rows": [
