    {
      "page_title": "Embedding vs Hand-Rolled Two-Phase Commit",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Embed First, Skip The Txn",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "await orders.updateOne({ _id: id }, {",
            "  $push: { items: line },",
            "  $inc: { total: line.price }",
            "})  // one doc: atomic, no session"
          ],
          "note": "one document needs no transaction"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Manual Two-Phase Commit"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "State",
            "App does"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "initial",
              "record the transfer"
            ],
            [
              "pending",
              "apply to both docs"
            ],
            [
              "applied",
              "clear pending marks"
            ],
            [
              "done",
              "transfer finished"
            ],
            [
              "canceling",
              "undo on failure"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "Why 4.0 mattered",
          "text": "The hand-rolled pattern leaked `pending` state into every read. Native transactions moved that bookkeeping into the server."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What A Session Is"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A `ServerSession` is server-side context keyed by a UUID",
            "That UUID travels as `lsid` on every command",
            "It carries the transaction number and transaction state",
            "No session, no transaction — the API refuses"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Reach for ==embedding== before any session",
            "Use a transaction only when two docs must move together",
            "Budget the extra commit round-trip when you do"
          ],
          "style": "checkbox"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Sessions: The Container for Everything",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const session = client.startSession()",
            "// ClientSession, lazily bound to a pooled one",
            "await coll.insertOne(doc, { session })",
            "// omit { session } and this runs OUTSIDE"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Silent killer",
          "text": "Every operation must pass the session object explicitly. Forget it on one line and that write commits on its own — abort will not roll it back."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Lifecycle"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "startSession",
              "bind from pool",
              "ops with session",
              "endSession",
              "back to pool"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "End it: `session.endSession()` in a `finally`",
            "Or let `withSession` / `withTransaction` do it for you",
            "Pooling makes `startSession` cheap — do not hoard one"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Reaping And Numbering"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Knob",
            "Default",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "localLogicalSessionTimeoutMinutes",
              "30",
              "idle session reaped"
            ],
            [
              "reap w/ open txn",
              "—",
              "transaction aborted"
            ],
            [
              "txnNumber",
              "1..n",
              "per transaction"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`txnNumber` increments per transaction inside one session",
            "That number makes a resent command idempotent",
            "Which is exactly what makes commit retries safe"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Session Rules",
          "items": [
            "One transaction per session at a time",
            "No nesting, no parallel ops on one session",
            "Never share a session across concurrent requests",
            "A session from client A throws if used on client B"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Not Just Transactions"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Sessions also power retryable writes",
            "And causal consistency, with no transaction in sight",
            "Both work on a plain session you never start a txn on"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "db.serverStatus().transactions",
            "// currentActive, currentInactive, totalAborted",
            "db.aggregate([{ $listLocalSessions: {} }])"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Writing a Transaction: The Core API",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Three Primitives",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`session.startTransaction()` opens the snapshot",
            "`session.commitTransaction()` applies everything",
            "`session.abortTransaction()` rolls everything back"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "try {",
            "  session.startTransaction()",
            "  await coll.insertOne(doc, { session })",
            "  await session.commitTransaction()",
            "} catch (e) { await session.abortTransaction() }"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Prefer The Callback"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "await session.withTransaction(async () => {",
            "  await orders.insertOne(o, { session })",
            "  await stock.updateOne(q, u, { session })",
            "}, { readConcern: { level: 'snapshot' } })"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`withTransaction` retries TransientTransactionError itself",
            "And retries UnknownTransactionCommitResult on the commit",
            "The callback may run more than once — keep it idempotent",
            "No side effects outside the database inside that callback",
            "Never swallow errors there: the driver needs them to retry"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Options Set At Start"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Option",
            "Note"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "readConcern",
              "snapshot or majority"
            ],
            [
              "writeConcern",
              "applies to the commit"
            ],
            [
              "readPreference",
              "primary only"
            ],
            [
              "maxCommitTimeMS",
              "bounds the commit"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "No secondary reads",
          "text": "`readPreference` inside a transaction must be primary. Secondary reads in transactions are not supported at all."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Visibility"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Other sessions see the pre-transaction state until commit",
            "Your own reads DO see your uncommitted writes",
            "Commit lands atomically as one oplog entry or applyOps chain",
            "Abort discards every write the transaction made"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "{ w: 'majority', j: true, wtimeout: 5000 }",
            "// w  = node count or replica-set tag",
            "// j  = fsync the journal before replying",
            "// wtimeout = ms before an error is raised"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Allowed Operations & Write Concern Levels",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Allowed vs Not"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Allowed",
                "lines": [
                  "insert / update / delete",
                  "findAndModify",
                  "most aggregation stages"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Refused",
                "lines": [
                  "index builds",
                  "`$out` and `$merge`",
                  "listCollections, count"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Two Hard Edges",
          "items": [
            "Use `countDocuments()`, never `count()`, in a txn",
            "Implicit collection create is an error pre-4.4",
            "`withTransaction` gives up after 120s wall-clock",
            "So a hand-rolled retry loop must be bounded too"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Question It Answers",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "How many nodes acknowledged before the driver returns",
            "Nothing to do with what you can read — that is read concern",
            "Three fields only: `w`, `j`, `wtimeout`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Levels"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "w",
            "Waits for",
            "Risk"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "0",
              "nothing",
              "no ack, no errors"
            ],
            [
              "1",
              "primary only",
              "failover rolls it back"
            ],
            [
              "majority",
              "quorum",
              "slowest, survives election"
            ],
            [
              "'multiDC'",
              "tag set",
              "e.g. two data centres"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`w: 'majority'` is the default since MongoDB 5.0",
            "`w: 0` is fire-and-forget — throwaway telemetry only",
            "Custom tag sets are defined in the replica set config"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Journalling"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`j: true` forces a journal fsync on each acknowledging node",
            "`writeConcernMajorityJournalDefault` decides if majority implies it",
            "`majority` + `j: true` is safest and slowest",
            "Latency tracks the slowest node needed to form the majority"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "wtimeout does not abort",
          "text": "It only stops the driver waiting. The write is still in the oplog and may replicate to a majority seconds after you got the error."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "session.startTransaction({",
            "  writeConcern: { w: 'majority', j: true }",
            "})  // one durability decision for the lot"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Write Concern in Transactions, Tag Sets & wtimeout",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Inside A Transaction"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The write concern applies to the COMMIT, not to each op",
            "Setting write concern on an op inside a txn is an error",
            "Set it on the transaction options or on commitTransaction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "await coll.insertOne(doc, {",
            "  session,",
            "  writeConcern: { w: 1 }  // ERROR",
            "})"
          ],
          "note": "per-op write concern refused in txn"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Defining A Tag Set"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "cfg = rs.conf()",
            "cfg.settings.getLastErrorModes =",
            "  { multiDC: { dc: 2 } }",
            "rs.reconfig(cfg)  // w: 'multiDC'"
          ],
          "note": "members need a dc tag"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Each member carries a tag such as `{ dc: 'east' }`",
            "`{ dc: 2 }` = acks from ==two distinct== `dc` values",
            "Survives losing a whole data centre, at WAN latency"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Handling wtimeout"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Error arrives as a write concern error, not a failed write",
            "Do NOT blindly re-insert — the write may already be there",
            "Re-read or use an idempotent upsert to reconcile"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`w: 3` on a two-node set — blocks, then errors",
            "`w: 0` on payments — failures vanish silently",
            "Per-op `writeConcern` inside a transaction"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "ink": "red"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Read Concern: The Five Levels",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Failure Modes",
          "items": [
            "`w` above the data-bearing voting count never satisfies",
            "It blocks until `wtimeout`, then errors",
            "readConcern `majority` is meaningless if writes never reach one"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Freshness vs Durability",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "How fresh, and how rollback-proof, must this read be",
            "`local` is the default outside transactions",
            "`majority` is the default inside transactions"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Five Levels"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Level",
            "Sees",
            "Cost"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "local",
              "node's latest",
              "fast, rollbackable"
            ],
            [
              "available",
              "skips orphan filter",
              "lowest latency"
            ],
            [
              "majority",
              "majority-acked",
              "durable, bit stale"
            ],
            [
              "snapshot",
              "point in time",
              "consistent across docs"
            ],
            [
              "linearizable",
              "all acked writes",
              "freshest, slowest"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`available` can hand back orphaned documents on a shard",
            "`linearizable` is primary-only and single-document",
            "It must be paired with `maxTimeMS` or it can hang",
            "`snapshot` works in txns and causally consistent sessions"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "majority vs linearizable"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "majority",
                "lines": [
                  "may read a lagging node",
                  "no extra round-trip",
                  "never rolled back"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "linearizable",
                "lines": [
                  "proves it is primary",
                  "pays a no-op write",
                  "one document only"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Snapshot Inside A Txn"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "session.startTransaction({",
            "  readConcern: { level: 'snapshot' }",
            "})  // same query twice = same answer"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Snapshot Age Limits",
          "items": [
            "`transactionLifetimeLimitSeconds` default 60",
            "WiredTiger history store holds the old versions",
            "Old snapshot = cache pressure, then an abort"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Aggregation pipelines honour read concern",
            "Change streams honour it too",
            "Pick per query: local for feeds, majority for money"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Choosing a Read Concern Level",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Snapshot is what makes transaction reads repeatable",
            "One point-in-time view across every document and shard",
            "Set at start — it cannot be changed per operation"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "majority is not read-your-own-write",
          "text": "Reading with `majority` guarantees durability, not recency of your own write. You need a causally consistent session for that."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "A Linearizable Read"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "db.acc.find({ _id: 7 })",
            "  .readConcern('linearizable')",
            "  .maxTimeMS(2000)  // never omit"
          ],
          "note": "single document, primary only"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Pick A Level"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Need",
            "Level"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "activity feed",
              "local"
            ],
            [
              "shard analytics",
              "available"
            ],
            [
              "account balance",
              "majority"
            ],
            [
              "multi-doc report",
              "snapshot"
            ],
            [
              "lock / leader check",
              "linearizable"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Per-op `readConcern` inside a txn — set it at start",
            "`linearizable` without `maxTimeMS` — may block forever",
            "`majority` reads while writes use `w: 1`"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Majority needs the history",
          "text": "`majority` and `snapshot` reads are served from older versions — WiredTiger keeps them until the majority point advances."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Snapshot Isolation and the Write Conflict",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "MVCC Underneath",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "WiredTiger keeps multiple versions of every document",
            "A transaction reads from a snapshot at its start timestamp",
            "Readers never block writers; writers never block readers",
            "No shared read locks, unlike lock-based SQL engines"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Write Conflict"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "// another commit touched this doc after",
            "// your snapshot began:",
            "WriteConflict (code 112)",
            "// surfaces as TransientTransactionError"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Where",
            "What happens"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "outside a txn",
              "WiredTiger retries, invisibly"
            ],
            [
              "inside a txn",
              "whole transaction aborts"
            ],
            [
              "with withTransaction",
              "callback re-runs from scratch"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Anti-pattern",
          "text": "Every order transaction bumping one global `stats` document. Each order now serialises behind the last — the hot doc becomes the throughput ceiling."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Transactions on hot documents degrade badly under load",
            "A shared counter or one inventory row is the classic trap",
            "Spread the contention: shard the counter into buckets"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Snapshot ≠ Serializable"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The guarantee is snapshot isolation, not serializability",
            "Write skew survives it and bites in production"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "// T1 and T2 both read: 2 doctors on call",
            "// T1 removes Alice. T2 removes Bob.",
            "// Different docs, no conflict. Both commit.",
            "// Invariant 'one on call' is now broken."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Fixing Write Skew",
          "items": [
            "Force a shared write target so a conflict is detected",
            "Or encode the rule as a unique index constraint",
            "Or move the invariant into a single document"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Contrast: PostgreSQL"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Postgres default",
                "lines": [
                  "READ COMMITTED",
                  "snapshot per statement",
                  "SERIALIZABLE opt-in"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "MongoDB txn",
                "lines": [
                  "snapshot isolation",
                  "snapshot per transaction",
                  "no serializable mode"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Intent Locks & Causal Consistency",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Locks Still Exist"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Transactions take intent locks on collections and databases",
            "A DDL operation can block a txn, or be blocked by one",
            "`maxTransactionLockRequestTimeoutMillis` defaults to 5ms",
            "Past that wait the transaction aborts rather than queue"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Guarantee",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Operations are observed in an order respecting cause and effect",
            "Concrete need: write to primary, then read from a secondary",
            "Without it that read can legally miss your own write"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const s = client.startSession({",
            "  causalConsistency: true  // on by default",
            "})",
            "await coll.findOne(q, { session: s })"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How It Works"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "write on primary",
              "reply carries operationTime",
              "driver stores it",
              "next read sends afterClusterTime",
              "secondary waits, then serves"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Every response carries an `operationTime` and a cluster time",
            "The driver echoes them back as `afterClusterTime`",
            "The node holds the read until its oplog has applied that far"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Four Guarantees"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Read your own writes",
            "Monotonic reads — never go backwards in time",
            "Monotonic writes — your writes apply in order",
            "Writes follow reads — a write lands after what it read"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Needs majority",
          "text": "The full guarantee requires readConcern `majority`. With `local` the causally-read data can still be rolled back after an election."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Session Scoped"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Only holds within ONE session",
            "Two sessions are unrelated unless you propagate cluster time",
            "`w: 0` writes are excluded — there is no operationTime to wait on"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Across Services"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const t = s.getClusterTime()  // send in API",
            "const o = s.operationTime",
            "s2.advanceClusterTime(t)  // receiving side",
            "s2.advanceOperationTime(o)"
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "MongoDB gives you atomicity for free inside one document — the moment you need two, you inherit sessions, snapshots, write conflicts and a two-phase commit. This handbook is the whole path: the core API, read and write concern, why snapshot isolation still lets write skew through, what changes on a sharded cluster, and the two error labels that decide whether you retry the transaction or just the commit. Save it for the day a transfer has to land on both sides.",
